(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form A","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which epidermolysis bullosa subtype carries the highest mortality?","choices":["Epidermolysis bullosa simplex, especially the localised palmoplantar form","Dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa, especially the dominant form","Epidermolysis bullosa acquisita, the autoimmune acquired form","Junctional epidermolysis bullosa, especially the Herlitz subtype"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is why early palliative care integration is appropriate in severe Herlitz disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which androgen is chiefly responsible for male-pattern hair loss?","choices":["Dihydrotestosterone","Testosterone acting directly on the follicle","Oestradiol","Cortisol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — male-pattern hair loss is an androgen-dependent trait with a strong paternal influence on risk. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 145"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed as a barrier to treatment and medication adherence?","choices":["Ready access to transportation","Cost and affordability of medications or services","High health literacy","Strong communication and follow-up by the care team"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cost and affordability heads the list of barriers carried over from Patient-Centered Healthcare. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which three cue sources feed the start of the hypothetico-deductive sequence?","choices":["Ask, advise, and assess","Published guidelines, a literature review, and the cost data for each test","Risk, benefit, and likelihood of success","Initial history, initial examination, and screening diagnostic testing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The sequence opens with initial history cues, initial examination cues and screening diagnostic testing cues. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Where is nevus simplex most common, and what makes it more noticeable?","choices":["Head and neck; crying","Trunk; exposure to sunlight","Extremities; cold temperature","Face and upper trunk; pregnancy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — pink to erythematous blanchable macules or patches, single or multiple. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What distinguishes an ulceration from an erosion?","choices":["An ulceration involves only the epidermis and always heals without scarring, while an erosion reaches the dermis or deeper","An ulceration is full-thickness loss extending to the dermis or deeper; an erosion loses only the epidermis","An ulceration is linear and an erosion is round","An ulceration is painful and an erosion is painless"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — depth is the discriminator, and it determines whether the lesion can scar. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which conditions are named in the differential for scabies?","choices":["Atopic dermatitis, body and pubic lice, other arthropod bites, dermatitis herpetiformis, fungal infection and psoriasis","Cellulitis, erysipelas, necrotizing fasciitis and a deep vein thrombosis of the whole affected lower leg","Lentigo maligna, seborrheic keratosis, café-au-lait macule and freckles","Bullous pemphigoid, pemphigus, thermal burn and herpes zoster infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — all can produce an intensely itchy eruption. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is the dimple sign, and which lesion shows it?","choices":["The lesion blanches completely under direct downward pressure; cherry angioma","The lesion transmits a wave-like fluid shift on palpation; pilonidal abscess","The lesion expresses pasty material through a central pore; epidermoid cyst","The lesion retracts beneath the skin surface with lateral compression; dermatofibroma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — this is the physical sign the lecture asks about directly. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man has vitiligo affecting roughly 20% of his body surface. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Phototherapy with narrow band ultraviolet B, ideally combined with topical therapy","Topical corticosteroid alone applied twice daily to all the affected areas","Surgical tissue or cellular grafting to repopulate the depigmented areas","PUVA phototherapy, which is preferred to narrow band ultraviolet B here"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — narrow band ultraviolet B is preferred over PUVA, which raises skin cancer risk. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which team must be involved BEFORE immunosuppression is reduced in iatrogenic Kaposi sarcoma?","choices":["The transplant team","Medical oncology, who direct all systemic and immunosuppressive decisions","Palliative care","Pulmonology or gastroenterology"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the deck lists them explicitly as a referral before reducing immunosuppression. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A child has genital molluscum lesions. What does the deck say about interpreting this?","choices":["It confirms sexual abuse, so a report should be filed on the location alone","It excludes sexual abuse, because genital lesions in children are always autoinoculated","It requires no assessment at all, because genital lesions are expected in children","It requires context-sensitive assessment, because location alone does not prove abuse"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — location alone is not proof. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 133"},{"q":"A 21-year-old asks why his pityriasis versicolor keeps returning each summer. Which factors does the lecture say make it more common?","choices":["Occlusive footwear, nail trauma, and peripheral vascular disease","Heat, humidity, oily skin, sweating, immunosuppression, or corticosteroid exposure","Cold, dry air, low sebum production, and frequent bathing with antibacterial soap","Obesity, incontinence, immobility, and recent antibiotic use in hospital"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — six named factors. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 15-year-old with moderate acne has a family history of keloids. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Avoid all topical acne therapy, since it provokes fibrous overgrowth","Treat the acne early and appropriately","Wait until the acne resolves on its own before starting any treatment","Begin silicone sheeting over the acne-prone areas now"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — acne is one of the named keloid triggers, and early appropriate treatment greatly increases the chance of scar-free healing. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"How is a poorly demarcated lesion described?","choices":["Raised above the surrounding skin surface","Irregular or blotchy in appearance, without well-defined borders","Smaller than one centimetre in every dimension measured","Present in more than one body region"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — as against a well demarcated lesion, which has clearly defined borders. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A patient cannot take terbinafine for confirmed onychomycosis. Which alternative does the lecture name, and what does it say about fluconazole?","choices":["Fluconazole is the alternative; itraconazole is off label in the United States","Griseofulvin is the alternative; fluconazole is first-line in the United States","Nystatin is the alternative; fluconazole is contraindicated in nail disease","Itraconazole is an alternative; fluconazole is off label in the United States"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — both points come from the same slide. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which points should the conversation with a hyperhidrosis patient cover?","choices":["That a single course of treatment produces a permanent cure","That the condition is rare and few treatment options exist","That it is not about poor hygiene or anxiety, with moisture-wicking clothing suggested and realistic expectations set","That improved washing and a reduction in stress will resolve all of the sweating within a few months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — botulinum toxin needs repeating every three to six months and can be cost-prohibitive. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"You suspect a basal cell carcinoma on a patient's shoulder. Which is the most appropriate diagnostic step, and why does it matter which subtype comes back?","choices":["Shave or punch biopsy, because the HISTOLOGIC subtype determines behaviour and dictates treatment selection","Shave or punch biopsy, because the CLINICAL subtype determines behaviour and dictates treatment selection","Wide local excision with 1 cm margins, because tissue diagnosis and treatment are achieved together","Sentinel lymph node biopsy, because nodal status determines treatment selection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — histology, not clinical appearance, drives the plan. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Head lice have recurred after two pediculicide courses. Which approach is described?","choices":["A multimodal approach combining physical methods with adjuvant chemical therapy","A third course of the same pediculicide at double the concentration","Occlusive home remedies such as mayonnaise or mineral oil alone","Oral ivermectin given as a single dose to the whole household"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — increasing resistance is the stated reason. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Besides sun exposure and immunosuppression, which risk factors does the deck list for squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia and human immunodeficiency virus only","Red tattoo ink, laser treatment and cryotherapy","Chronic wounds, scars or prior radiation fields; certain genetic diseases; and mucosal or genital disease","Tanning bed use, fair skin that burns easily, male sex, and outdoor occupation without protection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the three additional categories as listed. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How is a suspected squamous cell carcinoma diagnosed, and what must the sample achieve?","choices":["Shave biopsy only, since deeper sampling is thought to risk seeding the tumour into the dermis","Clinical diagnosis alone, with biopsy reserved for recurrence","Dermoscopy, with biopsy only if dermoscopy is inconclusive","Shave, punch or excisional biopsy sampling sufficient depth to separate in situ from invasive disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — depth is the point, because in situ and invasive carry different treatment, margins and risk. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man is being treated for Rocky Mountain spotted fever and asks how long the course will last. Which is the most appropriate answer?","choices":["Amoxicillin three times daily for a course of ten to fourteen days","Azithromycin once daily for a course of five days, then reassessment","Doxycycline 100 mg by mouth every twelve hours for five to ten days","Doxycycline 100 mg by mouth every twelve hours for four to six weeks"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the same agent is used in pregnancy, and children get a weight-based dose. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"How soon after the injury does each of these scars appear, and what happens with time?","choices":["Both develop within four weeks of the injury and both flatten over the following year","Both develop months after the trauma and neither improves without treatment","Hypertrophic scars develop soon after surgery and usually improve with time; keloids may develop months after the trauma and rarely improve","Hypertrophic scars develop months after the trauma and rarely improve; keloids develop soon after surgery and usually improve"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — timing and trajectory both separate them. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 44-year-old bartender has had swollen, tender nail folds on several fingers for four months, with thickened discoloured nails and cuticles that have separated from the nail plate. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Onychomycosis","Nail psoriasis","Chronic paronychia","Acute paronychia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — an inflammatory reaction to irritants over at least six weeks, with Candida albicans commonest. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"A 61-year-old kidney transplant recipient develops Kaposi sarcoma. Which is the most appropriate management step, and what caution applies?","choices":["Reduce immunosuppressive medication doses where clinically feasible, coordinating with the transplant team before making any change","Reduce immunosuppressive medication doses immediately without involving the transplant team, since the malignancy takes priority","Begin or optimise antiretroviral therapy, since immune restoration is the cornerstone of treatment","Begin liposomal doxorubicin and paclitaxel first, since iatrogenic disease does not respond to dose reduction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the transplant team must be involved before doses change. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A patient has recurrent, extensive candidal intertrigo. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Switch to a systemic corticosteroid to control the inflammation","Refer for surgical excision of the affected skin folds","Evaluate for diabetes and immunosuppression","Repeat the same topical course for a further six months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — recurrent or extensive disease prompts this. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man has a two-month history of intense itching followed by blisters on the trunk, axillae and inner thighs. Examination shows numerous tense bullae 1 to 3 cm across on urticarial bases; rubbing adjacent normal skin does not cause it to shear away. There is no oral involvement. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pemphigus vulgaris","Dyshidrotic eczema","Allergic contact dermatitis","Bullous pemphigoid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tense bullae, a negative Nikolsky sign, an itchy prodrome and onset after 60 are the picture. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"What is the relationship between naevus number and melanoma in dysplastic naevus syndrome?","choices":["Naevus number has no bearing at all on melanoma risk","Only the single largest naevus determines the overall risk","The higher the number of naevi, the higher the risk of melanoma","The higher the number of naevi, the lower the risk of melanoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — over one hundred by adolescence defines the syndrome. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is the earliest sign of an infantile hemangioma?","choices":["A pink to erythematous irregular macule that becomes more noticeable when the baby cries","A bright red exophytic papule with a moist surface that bleeds after minor trauma","Blanching of the involved skin, then fine telangiectasias, then a red or crimson macule","A flat well-circumscribed blanchable patch that darkens with crying, fever or overheating"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — about half are present at birth as a prominent cutaneous mark. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has a firm, movable, round nodule on his upper back with a small central pore that expresses cream-coloured pasty material. He asks whether it is a sebaceous cyst full of oil. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["It is mucin extruded from a nearby joint","The contents are keratin, not sebum","Yes, it is a blocked sebaceous gland filled with sebum","It is a collection of lipid-laden macrophages"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is often called a sebaceous cyst because the material looks like sebum, but the deck states outright that it is not one. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 5-year-old with naevus spilus is found to have a vascular anomaly on further examination. Which is the most appropriate interpretation?","choices":["Naevus spilus can be associated with anomalies of vascular, central nervous system or connective tissue origin","The vascular anomaly is unrelated, since naevus spilus has no described systemic associations at all","The vascular anomaly indicates neurocutaneous melanosis and brain imaging is needed","The vascular anomaly indicates a familial cancer syndrome and genetic testing is needed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lesion itself rarely progresses to melanoma. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man with suspected body lice has no visible organisms on skin examination. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Wet comb the scalp with water and conditioner using a fine-toothed nit comb","Pluck a hair from the affected area and examine it under the microscope for nits","Scrape an unexcoriated papule with mineral oil and examine the smear microscopically","Examine the seams of his clothing for nits and shake the clothing over white paper"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the lice will move onto the paper where they can be seen. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has an actinic keratosis on the cheek that has become indurated, tender and has bled twice. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Biopsy the lesion to exclude invasive squamous cell carcinoma","Apply liquid nitrogen with two to three freeze-thaw cycles","Begin imiquimod 5% twice weekly for a sixteen-week course","Begin diclofenac 3% gel and reassess it in three months"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — induration, tenderness and bleeding are the concerning features named for progression. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"A 44-year-old with psoriasis has painful, stiff fingers each morning for over an hour, easing with activity. Examination shows a swollen sausage-shaped third digit, and the nails show pitting and separation of the plate from the bed. Which radiographic finding would you expect?","choices":["Subchondral sclerosis with marginal osteophytes","Periarticular osteopenia with symmetric erosions","Pencil-in-a-cup deformity","Bamboo spine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — psoriatic arthritis, associated with HLA-B27, affecting the distal and proximal interphalangeal joints while sparing the metacarpophalangeals. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 161"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man has early acute paronychia with erythema and tenderness but no fluctuance. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Immediate removal of the nail plate under local block","Warm water soaks for twenty minutes three times daily","Incision and drainage under digital block anaesthesia","A broad-spectrum topical antifungal applied twice daily"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Incision and drainage is reserved for severe cases with purulent collection. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with vitiligo has been using a topical corticosteroid on her eyelids for several months. Which risks should be checked for?","choices":["Tooth staining and hepatotoxicity from the agent","Skin atrophy and raised intraocular pressure","Increased skin cancer risk and photosensitivity","Teratogenicity and the need for pregnancy testing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a topical calcineurin inhibitor suits areas where steroids cannot be applied. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What does pulsed dye laser do to a hypertrophic scar?","choices":["Reduces scar erythema by reducing neovascularization","Reduces scar height by cross-linking collagen fibers","Reduces pruritus by destroying cutaneous nerve endings","Reduces recurrence by removing the entire cyst capsule"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it also decreases scar height and improves texture. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"When should resistant dermatophytosis be suspected?","choices":["Only in immunocompromised patients","Widespread, intensely inflammatory, linked, or terbinafine-refractory","When the lesion clears centrally within two weeks of starting treatment","Whenever a potassium hydroxide preparation is negative"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — epidemiologically linked cases and failure of an adequate terbinafine course both count. Obtain species identification and susceptibility testing when available. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man with erysipelas of the face has no drug allergies. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Penicillin V, with symptomatic care, hydration and elevation of the affected part","Cephalexin, with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole added if purulence develops later","Mupirocin ointment, with oral dicloxacillin if the lesions become widespread","Clindamycin, which is reserved for patients who report a penicillin allergy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — prompt treatment matters because progression can be rapid. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"How do primary and secondary skin lesions differ in the depth they affect?","choices":["Primary lesions appear first in time while secondary lesions appear only after treatment has been started","Primary lesions affect the epidermis and superficial dermis; secondary lesions infiltrate the dermis or subcutaneous tissue","Primary lesions infiltrate the dermis and subcutaneous tissue; secondary lesions affect only the superficial epidermis","Primary lesions arise from infection while secondary lesions arise from an immune reaction to that infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Their combination determines the diagnostic category, also called the reaction pattern. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man has a 7 mm naevus with an indistinct border and a pebbly surface, with tan and brown areas within it, on the upper back. Which test establishes the diagnosis?","choices":["Wood's lamp","Serology","Biopsy","Dermoscopy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — dysplastic melanocytic naevus is diagnosed histologically. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"The parent of an 8-year-old treated for head lice asks when she can return to school. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["She must stay home until a repeat wet comb finds no nits and no live lice","She can return now — a no-nit policy is not recommended because of the school absence it causes","She must stay home until every single nit has been combed out of her hair completely","She must stay home for two full weeks after the treatment has been completed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — that position comes from the American Academy of Pediatrics. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Where is the eruption of dermatitis herpetiformis distributed?","choices":["On sun-exposed skin, sparing the chronically exposed face and hands","Symmetrically on the knees, elbows, buttocks and back","On the anterior shins bilaterally as tender nodules that do not ulcerate","On the face in the central third, sparing the nasolabial folds"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — intensely pruritic papules and vesicles that group in a herpetiform pattern. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"How is Kaposi sarcoma confirmed, and what supports the diagnosis on histology?","choices":["Biopsy of the largest lesion, with Epstein-Barr virus findings supporting it","Clinical appearance alone, since the violaceous colour is characteristic","Human immunodeficiency virus testing alone, since that establishes the whole context","Biopsy of a representative lesion, with human herpesvirus 8-associated findings supporting it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the virus links the histology back to the cause. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman on amiodarone develops photosensitivity, and the cardiologist cannot substitute the drug. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Oral hydroxychloroquine at 200 mg twice daily as a long-term measure","Oral antihistamines taken each morning through the summer months","Strict photoprotection with broad-spectrum SPF 50 and physical blockers","Prophylactic narrowband ultraviolet B phototherapy given each spring"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — zinc oxide and titanium dioxide are named where the agent cannot be withdrawn. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"When the diagnosis carries some uncertainty, what balance must the decision to treat take into account?","choices":["The clinician's experience against published guidelines","The benefit of treating a sick person against the risk of erroneously treating a well person or one with a different disorder","The speed of the test against its accuracy","The benefit of screening the wider population against the financial cost of confirmatory testing for each positive result"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. This is the lecture's statement of the risk-versus-benefit balance under diagnostic uncertainty. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has one seborrheic keratosis on the back that has become itchy and inflamed where his belt rubs. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Topical depigmenting agents applied over several months","Observation alone, since no treatment exists for these","Cryotherapy, though the lesion may recur afterwards","Excision with negative margins to prevent it recurring"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — asymptomatic lesions need no treatment at all. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman with primary hyperhidrosis says she has been told repeatedly that she just needs to wash more and relax. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Improved hygiene and stress reduction will resolve the sweating over time","The condition affects a very small minority and treatment options are limited","Sweating of this kind always indicates a serious underlying systemic illness","Primary hyperhidrosis is a medical condition rather than a problem of hygiene or anxiety"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the misconception is addressed directly, and quality-of-life impairment is comparable to severe psoriasis. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What does the lecture advise regarding where a patient sits on the continuum of behaviour change?","choices":["Refer every patient for specialist behavioural therapy","Apply the same standardised intervention regardless of where the patient sits on that continuum","Delay counselling until the patient is ready to change","Identify where they are and tailor interventions to their readiness and self-efficacy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture advises identifying the patient's position on the continuum and tailoring interventions to their readiness and self-efficacy. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 10-year-old boy is stung by a honeybee and the stinger is still visible in the skin. Which is the most appropriate immediate action?","choices":["Scrape it off with a card edge held parallel to the skin, as quickly as possible","Grasp it with fine forceps and withdraw it with steady traction along its axis","Apply ice for twenty minutes and remove it once the swelling has settled down","Leave it in place, since removal releases the remaining venom into the tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the barbed ovipositor keeps pumping venom while it remains impaled. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 47-year-old healthy woman has a small area of cellulitis on the forearm with minimal pain, no fever and no risk factors for serious illness. Which is the most appropriate diagnostic approach?","choices":["Blood cultures and a punch biopsy, since organism identification guides therapy","Plain films of the forearm, since underlying osteomyelitis must be excluded","Venous duplex ultrasound, since deep vein thrombosis must be excluded first","No workup, since the diagnosis is clinical in limited disease without systemic signs"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Serious infection warrants blood cultures, punch biopsy, blood count and creatine phosphokinase. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 27-year-old Black man has tender erythematous papules along the jawline and neck, several with a visible hair shaft at the centre. They appeared after he began shaving daily with a five-blade razor. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pseudofolliculitis barbae","Bacterial folliculitis of the beard area","Acne vulgaris affecting the jaw and neck","Tinea barbae from a dermatophyte infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a foreign body reaction to cut hair re-entering the skin in a shaved area. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What is the immunological basis of dermatitis herpetiformis?","choices":["Immunoglobulin G antibodies directed against the epidermal basement membrane zone at the dermal junction","Immunoglobulin G antibodies against desmoglein between the keratinocytes","A T-cell mediated destruction of melanocytes within the affected epidermis","Immunoglobulin A antibodies against epidermal transglutaminase, forming complexes that deposit in the dermal papillae"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — complement and neutrophils are then activated, producing subepidermal blisters. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"When does herpes zoster scar?","choices":["Only in patients who go on to develop postherpetic neuralgia, whatever the depth of the lesions","Only when antiviral therapy is started later than 72 hours after the rash appears","When deeper epidermal or dermal layers are compromised by excoriation or secondary infection","Always, because the virus itself destroys the basement membrane wherever the lesions appear on the skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it typically heals without visible sequelae otherwise. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with suspected necrotizing fasciitis is being prepared for theatre. Which laboratory studies should be sent?","choices":["Complete blood count with differential, chemistry, arterial blood gas, urinalysis, and blood and tissue cultures","Complete blood count and inflammatory markers only, since the remaining studies add nothing to management","Antistreptolysin O titre and throat culture, since Group A streptococcus is a common cause","No laboratory studies at all, since none of them changes the operative decision here"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — they are sent, but they must not delay the surgical intervention itself. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 128"},{"q":"A 39-year-old has a 4 mm pigmented lesion that is asymmetric with an irregular border and has darkened over three months. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Photograph and review in six months, because evolution over three months is too rapid for melanoma","Refer for sentinel lymph node biopsy before sampling the lesion","Biopsy or excise it, because smaller lesions can still be melanoma","Reassure and review in a year, because a lesion under 6 mm cannot be melanoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the 6 mm figure is a prompt, not a rule-out. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man with deeply pigmented skin has a warm, tender, swollen forearm. The examiner cannot appreciate any erythema. Which is the most appropriate approach?","choices":["Take a punch biopsy immediately, since clinical assessment cannot be relied on","Rely on palpation, distribution and secondary change, since erythema is an unreliable signal on darker skin","Conclude that inflammation is absent, since visible erythema is required in order to diagnose it","Perform a Wood's lamp examination to bring out the erythema in a dark room"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the finding is present but the colour cue is not. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 114"},{"q":"When should polymerase chain reaction be used in suspected herpes zoster, and from what?","choices":["Only in patients aged over 50 with typical disease; from paired serology rather than from the lesion itself","For atypical, disseminated, vaccine-modified or immunocompromised presentations; from vesicle fluid, scab, or cells from the lesion base","In every case before starting antivirals, whatever the presentation; from a blood sample rather than from the lesion or its vesicle fluid","Only after antiviral therapy has failed, and never at presentation; from a full-thickness skin biopsy rather than from vesicle fluid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — typical unilateral dermatomal vesicles are diagnosed clinically. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"How is dysplastic melanocytic naevus diagnosed and managed?","choices":["Diagnosis is made clinically; management is observation alone with sun protection counselling, since these lesions never progress to melanoma at all","Diagnosis is clinical; management is cryotherapy or quality-switched laser for cosmetic removal","Diagnosis is by Wood's lamp; management is phototherapy for extensive body surface involvement","Diagnosis is by biopsy; management is observation, biopsy of all changing or developing lesions, excision where melanoma is a concern, and sun protection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — melanoma risk rises with the number of naevi present. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Where is minoxidil primarily effective?","choices":["Over the entire scalp equally","Only in women","In the crown region of the scalp","At the frontal hairline"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it prevents further loss and may produce regrowth, working best with finasteride. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 148"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man whose two children developed hot tub folliculitis last month asks how to stop it happening again. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Shower thoroughly within thirty minutes of leaving the water on every occasion","Raise the water temperature above forty degrees to kill the responsible organism","Limit each session to twenty minutes and avoid submerging the trunk entirely","Maintain continuous water filtration, monitor disinfectant levels and change the water often"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Showering after contact does not prevent the infection. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 53"}]};
